From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Lebedev <sergey@lebedev.mobi>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:26:39 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] mips: econet: disarm the bootloader's CP0 Compare before
 VEIC dispatch

econet does not use CP0 Count/Compare as a clockevent source -- the HPT
peripheral driven from plat_time_init() is the real one -- so nothing in
the platform ever services a Compare match. The bootloader does use
Count/Compare for its own timing, and hands the kernel a Compare value that
has already elapsed, so Cause.TI reads set from the first instant of boot.

That is harmless under legacy IRQ dispatch, where the core's internal timer
condition is routed nowhere. Once CONFIG_MIPS_MT_SMP makes cpu_has_veic true
on this core, the CPU presents the same condition as a vectored interrupt as
soon as interrupts are enabled, landing in the do_default_vi() slot
trap_init() left behind -- only the econet INTC's own lines ever get real
vectors installed. The result is a "Caught unexpected vectored interrupt"
panic on the first local_irq_enable() of boot, reproducible on every boot of
an EN751221 board.

Disarm it in arch_init_irq(), before irqchip_init() can install any vector.
Any write to Compare clears Cause.TI as an architectural side effect, and
writing count - 1 puts the next match a full 32-bit wraparound away.

The same class of bug affects secondary VPEs from vsmp_smp_finish(); that
one is fixed separately in arch/mips/kernel/smp-mt.c.

Signed-off-by: Sergey Lebedev <sergey@lebedev.mobi>
---
--- a/arch/mips/econet/init.c
+++ b/arch/mips/econet/init.c
@@ -119,6 +119,32 @@ const char *get_system_type(void)
 /* 4. Initialize the IRQ subsystem */
 void __init arch_init_irq(void)
 {
+	/*
+	 * Disarm any CP0 Count/Compare condition inherited from the
+	 * bootloader before the vector table can see it. econet does not use
+	 * CP0 Compare as a clockevent source -- the HPT peripheral driven by
+	 * plat_time_init() below is the real one -- so nothing here ever
+	 * services it, while the bootloader does use Count/Compare for its
+	 * own timing and hands the kernel a Compare value that has already
+	 * elapsed. Cause.TI therefore reads set from the first instant of
+	 * boot.
+	 *
+	 * That is harmless under legacy IRQ dispatch, where nothing routes
+	 * the core's internal timer condition anywhere. Once CONFIG_MIPS_MT_SMP
+	 * makes cpu_has_veic true on this core, though, the CPU presents the
+	 * condition as a vectored interrupt as soon as interrupts are enabled,
+	 * landing in the do_default_vi() slot trap_init() left behind -- only
+	 * the econet INTC's own lines ever get real vectors installed. The
+	 * result is a "Caught unexpected vectored interrupt" panic on the
+	 * first local_irq_enable() of boot, every time.
+	 *
+	 * Any write to Compare clears Cause.TI as an architectural side
+	 * effect; writing count - 1 puts the next match a full 32-bit
+	 * wraparound away.
+	 */
+	if (cpu_has_veic)
+		write_c0_compare(read_c0_count() - 1);
+
 	irqchip_init();
 }
 
